 id:'FX-02',
 title:'The legacy getRate() still cannot fail, still answers for the wrong tenant, and is still reachable',
 capabilityIds:['SUP-06'],
 classification:'WRONG_BEHAVIOR',
 kind:'fresh defect — shared service, outside the frozen capability scope',
 status:'OPEN',
 roles:['Finance'],
 stages:['Finance / Collection'],
 authority:'Finance FX (core ExchangeRateService) — the legacy resolution path left in place beside the governed one',
 currentBehavior:'FX-01 cut every money path away from getRate(); it did not change getRate(), by instruction. The function remains exactly as the audit found it. It cannot fail: an ungoverned pair returns a hardcoded peg, and a currency outside the five-currency union cross-rates through USD where the unknown leg resolves to parity, so ANY unrecognised currency converts at the USD peg 3.6725 — JPY, KRW, INR alike. EUR and GBP float but are pinned to the constants 1.0900 and 1.2700, so their answers are guesses with no effective date and no approver. A database error is caught, logged and followed by a peg, so an outage and a missing rate produce the same confident number. And inMemoryRates is keyed FROM:TO with NO TENANT, so a rate registered by one tenant answers another tenant’s question — a tenant-isolation breach in the resolution path, distinct from the valuation error. It is reachable through exactly one surface: GET /api/v1/finance/fx/convert, which calls both getRate and convert. Nothing that books money reaches it any more, which is why this is severe rather than critical; an amount converted there is still a number a person may act on.',
 expectedOperationalBehavior:'One FX authority. Either the legacy path is removed with its remaining caller moved to the governed resolver, or it is retained deliberately as a clearly-labelled indicative conversion that states it is not governed — decided, not inherited.',
 evidence:'FRESH audit following the FX-01 remediation: core/src/finance/exchange-rate.service.ts getRate/convert/getDefaultPeg/inMemoryRates; sole remaining consumer apps/api/src/finance/fx.controller.ts:47-48. The tenant leak and the peg fallback are both asserted as CURRENT behaviour in core/src/finance/governed-rate.test.ts, which contrasts them against the governed resolver rather than merely describing them.',
 severity:'HIGH',
 remediationDependency:'None, and it BLOCKS NOTHING. SUP-06 is listed as a RELATED capability, not a hard dependency: modules/procurement has no FX coupling at all — it imports ExchangeRateService nowhere — and when SUP-06 normalises it will call resolveGovernedRate directly, never this endpoint. COM-05 and MGT-10 were dropped from the linkage because FX-01 already cut AP, AR and both revaluations away from the legacy path, so nothing they depend on touches it. SUP-06 may proceed before, during or after this record; they are independent.',
 acceptanceProof:'SINGLE TRACK. An earlier draft of this record offered "keep the endpoint and label it indicative" as an equal option, and that was wrong: a label is a documentation fix for a tenant-isolation defect, and it would have legitimised the untenanted cache rather than removed it. AURA either converts currency under governance or does not offer conversion. PHASE 1, CONSUMER PROOF (DONE): GET /api/v1/finance/fx/convert has ONE first-party consumer, the Convert widget in apps/web/components/fx-client.tsx reached through a BFF route; NO server-side caller; NO hand-maintained contract — packages/sdk/src/generated.ts is auto-generated from the OpenAPI document and its fxConvert method has zero callers; and no evidence of any external integration. PHASE 2, WHAT THIS RECORD CLOSES ON: the endpoint resolves through resolveGovernedRate and nothing else — tenant-scoped, no peg, no hardcoded EUR/GBP constant, no implicit cross-rate, an unsupported currency refused, a lookup failure reported as a failure rather than as absence, and the governed rate’s effective date, source and row id returned with the answer; then getRate, convert, getDefaultPeg, inMemoryRates and registerInMemoryRate are DELETED, proved by zero remaining callers and by a fitness test that fails if any of them is reintroduced; and the browser shows a finance user an ungoverned pair refused in the domain’s own words on the very page where rates are set — which is the point, because that screen today lists the governed rates and converts at a hardcoded constant at the same time. Conversion at a market or indicative rate is NOT in scope and must not be smuggled in under this record: it is a separate capability needing a provider, a source, a timestamp and freshness semantics of its own.',
