 currentBehavior:"CLOSED ON THE SHAPE, NOT THE STRING. The record read “an update-only actor can set status=approved”, and that value had been refused by a domain guard for some time while the record stayed open — because refusing one value in a list left the shape that produced it exactly as it was. Executed against the running API as a Buyer holding `procurement.po.update` and NOT `.approve`: issuing a 4,000 order to a supplier succeeded with no approval fact anywhere, cancelling a Director-approved 90,000 order succeeded and REVERSED ITS COMMITTED COST in the ledger, and closing one succeeded. A GENERIC MUTATION PATH MUST NEVER OWN A GOVERNED LIFECYCLE TRANSITION, and that is what is fixed. `PATCH /purchase-orders/:id/status` now refuses every status and names where each act went. ISSUE is its own command reachable ONLY from `approved` at any value — the auto-approve tier means the approval is TAKEN BY THE MATRIX and recorded with a basis, a level and a time (migration 0360), not that the step is skipped. CANCEL is its own command: it requires a reason, checks the approval authority on WHAT IS BEING REVERSED, and reverses only the REMAINING cancellable commitment — an order part delivered has part become real, and the ledger now receives that figure rather than the order's value. It refuses outright where nothing remains (a delivered order is closed, not cancelled) and where an order has been invoiced beyond what arrived, rather than compounding an unresolved position. CLOSE is deliberately NOT a variant of cancel: operational completion, with its own permission, no approval-authority check, and conditions of its own — nothing outstanding to deliver, nothing unbilled, nothing overbilled. Both hold the order's row `FOR UPDATE` and decide INSIDE the transaction that writes, so a delivery landing mid-decision cannot make the decision stale. A position that cannot be read REFUSES both acts rather than reading as “nothing has happened”. The Buyer holds none of the three permissions, which is the role description (“prepares purchase orders without approving their own order”) finally becoming true.",
